WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.640
It's packed with the most unstoppable assassin&nbsp;
action yet, but how will fans feel about that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:10.895
nearly three-hour runtime? Here's the best&nbsp;
and worst things about "John Wick: Chapter 4."&nbsp;

00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:13.200
One element of the "John Wick" series that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.880
prove divisive is the way it handles pedestrians.&nbsp;
Shootouts and fight scenes throughout the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:23.400
franchise often take place in crowded locations&nbsp;
packed with extras, but all these onlookers never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:28.200
react to the bloodshed happening right in front of&nbsp;
them. This isn't an issue in the first film, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:32.340
all of the sequels take a more heightened-reality&nbsp;
approach to the crowds of civilians.&nbsp;

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:36.360
Several people can get gunned down close&nbsp;
enough to them to be in the splash zone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.920
and the civilians will just keep on dancing&nbsp;
to the music even as bullets fly. For some,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:45.600
this is part of the campy fun of the series.&nbsp;
For others, it is one step too far removed from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:50.940
reality to avoid breaking immersion and making&nbsp;
the world feel artificial. It can begin to seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:55.260
like there are no normal people in the world&nbsp;
of "John Wick" and that everyone is an assassin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.260 --> 00:00:59.940
or works alongside assassins in the underworld.
"John Wick: Chapter 4" and the other sequels would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:04.380
benefit from the inclusion of more characters&nbsp;
outside of the assassination game to provide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:09.180
contrast. John Wick's deceased wife Helen and&nbsp;
Caine's daughter Mia are normal civilians, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:13.260
neither of them has a real presence. This issue&nbsp;
makes it harder to buy into the world of "John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.260 --> 00:01:16.079
Wick" as a functioning society with each sequel.

00:01:16.079 --> 00:01:17.555
"Amen." 

00:01:17.555 --> 00:01:19.440
After the relatively straightforward and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.940
simple first movie, the settings and backdrops of&nbsp;
the "John Wick" sequels have all been impressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:29.520
varied, and well-integrated into the action set&nbsp;
pieces. "Chapter 4" raises the bar in stunning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.620
fashion by turning the plot into a full-fledged&nbsp;
globe-spanning adventure. New York City remains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:39.660
an important location in this latest outing.&nbsp;
However, the plot also finds John Wick returning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:45.120
to the Sahara desert of "Chapter 3" to visit the&nbsp;
Elder, as well as traveling to Osaka, Berlin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.980
and even Paris for the showdown with the Marquis.
Adding to the effectiveness of the locations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.360
themselves is the way in which they are shot. The&nbsp;
prior "John Wick" movies are no slouches when it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:58.860
comes to their visual aesthetics, but "Chapter 4"&nbsp;
arguably has the best cinematography of the bunch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:03.960
The change from the steely, muted color palette of&nbsp;
the first movie to the increasingly more colorful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.600
sequels is also a welcome progression.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:10.200
The first three movies mostly line up when&nbsp;
it comes to the rules of the universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:14.880
but "Chapter 4" changes a few things up and asks&nbsp;
you to turn a blind eye to the discrepancies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:19.440
These issues might have something to do with&nbsp;
original screenwriter Derek Kolstad being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:23.460
completely uninvolved with "Chapter 4" for&nbsp;
the first time in the history of the series.&nbsp;

00:02:23.460 --> 00:02:28.680
One of the biggest inconsistencies is how easy&nbsp;
it is for John to travel this time around. A huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:33.420
part of "Chapter 3" is devoted to his struggle&nbsp;
to get from New York City to Casablanca. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.160
requires him to secure passage from the Ruska&nbsp;
Roma, which involves them tearing his ticket for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:42.420
a one-way trip. In "Chapter 4," John makes&nbsp;
the same international journey off-screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:46.800
with no mind paid to how he gets there.&nbsp;
He travels around the world without any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.000
difficulty -- despite the bounty on his head&nbsp;
being significantly higher than ever before.&nbsp;

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:56.340
It is also unclear why John needs to go to Berlin&nbsp;
to get the support of the Ruska Roma after he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.340 --> 00:03:00.900
previously met at their headquarters in New York&nbsp;
City. The complete absence of the Director is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:05.417
especially conspicuous when she seems like the&nbsp;
perfect person to solve John's specific problem.&nbsp;

00:03:05.417 --> 00:03:06.540
The "John Wick" movies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:11.580
always have strong soundtracks, mixing the series'&nbsp;
original score by Tyler Bates and Joel J. Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:16.740
with pre-existing music to great effect. Though&nbsp;
there are plenty of memorable needle-drop moments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:21.780
in the previous films, "Chapter 4" has arguably&nbsp;
the best overall soundtrack of the entire series.&nbsp;

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:26.880
Bates and Richard return as the main composers&nbsp;
and reprise the familiar themes while ensuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.080
the music continues to evolve and feel fresh.&nbsp;
The needle-drop moments between the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.420
score are a cut above the already-high bar set by&nbsp;
the series. Fans of French electronic music will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:41.460
be especially thrilled as tracks from popular&nbsp;
artists like Gesaffelstein and Justice show up.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.560
One of the best musical moments in the entire film&nbsp;
is actually a reprise of a song first utilized in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:52.020
the original "John Wick." The song "LED Spirals"&nbsp;
by Le Castle Vania makes a welcome return to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:56.340
soundtrack of "Chapter 4" -- after previously&nbsp;
scoring the centerpiece shootout that takes place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:58.739
in the Red Circle nightclub in the first movie.

00:03:58.739 --> 00:04:02.760
If there is anything that hurts the action&nbsp;
of "John Wick: Chapter 4," it is that John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:06.900
feels a bit too invincible at times. Part&nbsp;
of the joy of the first "John Wick" is how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:11.820
much pain he suffered and how wounded he was by&nbsp;
the end, and yet still came out on top. Giving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:16.020
John a bulletproof suit in "Chapter 2" runs&nbsp;
the risk of making him too indestructible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.020 --> 00:04:20.100
but the film finds a way to balance the&nbsp;
scales and keep the sense of danger intact.

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:25.020
By "Chapter 4," John begins to feel a bit&nbsp;
too superhuman. It really only becomes a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.020 --> 00:04:29.400
major distraction when it comes to the multiple&nbsp;
two- to three-story falls he takes and bounces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.480
back from like they're nothing. These land&nbsp;
in stark contrast to the fall he takes from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.480 --> 00:04:37.380
the roof of the Continental at the end of&nbsp;
"Chapter 3," which requires substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.760
time to rest and recover before he's ready&nbsp;
to fight again. John takes two huge falls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.440
of a comparable size in "Chapter 4," not&nbsp;
to mention the various other hits, stabs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.000
gunshots, and hundreds of stairs that he falls&nbsp;
down. Yet, he hardly bats an eye at all the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.320
physical trauma this time around. The stakes&nbsp;
of the action feel a little lower as a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.320 --> 00:04:58.589
though the very end of the film&nbsp;
course-corrects for the better.

00:04:58.589 --> 00:05:00.600
The "John Wick" series is almost as much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.160
about dogs as it is about action. The murder of&nbsp;
John's puppy in the original film is, of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:10.260
the straw that breaks the camel's back and kicks&nbsp;
off his quest for vengeance. Each subsequent movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:14.880
keeps canines as a part of the series' DNA.
In "Chapter 4" a new dog is brought into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:19.980
action, owned by the Tracker, aka Mr. Nobody.&nbsp;
We see this dog in some creative new ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:24.960
like running and leaping across the tops of cars&nbsp;
in a shootout set in front of the Arc de Triomphe.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.820
The best moment involving the dog is actually a&nbsp;
choice made by John. Though the dog and Mr. Nobody&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:34.380
have been trying to kill him, John saves the dog&nbsp;
when she is about to be killed. It is a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:39.253
moment of redemption for John after what happened&nbsp;
to Daisy all the way back in the first movie.&nbsp;

00:05:39.253 --> 00:05:41.580
"John Wick: Chapter 3" ends with a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:45.840
cliffhanger. After throwing away the freedom he&nbsp;
earned from the high table to defend Winston,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.520
John is double-crossed. Winston shoots him&nbsp;
and sends him off the roof of the Continental.&nbsp;

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:55.380
The first three "John Wick" movies all take place&nbsp;
within a short time span, each one beginning right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.380 --> 00:06:00.300
after the previous one ended. For the first time,&nbsp;
"Chapter 4" picks up following an extended gap in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.300 --> 00:06:04.500
time after John and the Bowery King are both&nbsp;
fully healed. Once John is back in action,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:10.140
he only has his sights set on the High Table, not&nbsp;
on Winston. When the two do cross paths, John lets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:15.060
Winston off the hook extremely easily, and the two&nbsp;
become chummy again and spend the rest of the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:19.860
working together. The promised vengeance against&nbsp;
Winston never happens, nor does Winston ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.860 --> 00:06:25.200
truly reckon with his act of betrayal. This is&nbsp;
particularly hard to buy given that one of John's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:29.860
defining character traits is that he can't let&nbsp;
things go and always needs to settle the score.&nbsp;

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.700
"What have you done?"

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:36.720
"Chapter 4" brings a focus back&nbsp;
to John's deceased wife, Helen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.440
in key moments that are quite effective and&nbsp;
help make the series feel like it is coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:45.300
full circle. Her presence is less prominent than&nbsp;
in the first movie but far more impactful than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.300 --> 00:06:49.620
it is in the other sequels. In one key scene,&nbsp;
John lights a candle and pays his respects to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:53.760
her before having a friendly conversation with&nbsp;
Caine about why. John says he doesn't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:58.020
she can hear him or feel his presence, but&nbsp;
he does it anyway because he could be wrong.&nbsp;

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:03.120
John's most vulnerable and human moment in the&nbsp;
entire film arrives when he tells Winston that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.120 --> 00:07:08.040
wants his epitaph to read "loving husband." He's&nbsp;
an infamous assassin with a legendary reputation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:13.020
yet he only wants to be remembered in relation&nbsp;
to Helen. And finally the quick flash of Helen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:17.160
in John's dying moments is a beautiful way&nbsp;
to tie it all together and provide a fitting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.160 --> 00:07:19.320
conclusion to Mr. Wick's journey.

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:24.060
The first "John Wick" movie and its first sequel&nbsp;
give John a clear goal to work toward in typically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:28.800
violent fashion. "Chapter 3" is the first time&nbsp;
where John's motivation starts to falter. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:33.000
"Chapter 4" is a step down in the motivation&nbsp;
department, as John doesn't really have a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.980
reason for the bloodshed at all this time around.&nbsp;
As characters say as much within the film. Winston&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:42.300
tells John that what he's doing is pointless&nbsp;
and won't accomplish anything, and he's right.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.300 --> 00:07:47.160
The Marquis calls John a man with nothing to live&nbsp;
for, nothing to die for, and nothing to kill for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.720
and he's also right. The film embraces this&nbsp;
emptiness in John, but it is a genuine problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:56.160
with the central figure. John barely feels&nbsp;
like a character anymore in "Chapter 4" and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.080
is mostly just a vessel for other characters&nbsp;
to exploit, try to kill, and get killed by.&nbsp;

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:03.720
"I'm going to need a gun."

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:09.000
"John Wick: Chapter 4" introduces many new&nbsp;
characters to the series, and they are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.780
great. Chidi, Akira, the harbinger, Shimazu, The&nbsp;
Tracker, Marquis, and especially Caine are all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.780 --> 00:08:20.040
fantastic additions to the "John Wick" world.
Caine even steals the show from John Wick and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.040 --> 00:08:24.000
nearly begins to feel like the main character&nbsp;
himself at points as he takes center stage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:27.480
for stretches where John is absent.&nbsp;
The post-credits scene teases that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:31.500
actually might become the protagonist of his own&nbsp;
spinoff at some point in the franchise's future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.140
which sounds fantastic in theory.

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:38.820
Each film in the "John Wick" series has gotten&nbsp;
progressively longer, but the biggest leap comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:44.100
with "John Wick: Chapter 4." This new installment&nbsp;
is nearly three hours long. On the plus side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.100 --> 00:08:48.000
the longer runtime means more action, and&nbsp;
the film fully delivers in that category.

00:08:48.000 --> 00:08:52.380
All the same, the hefty length is a clear&nbsp;
hindrance. Too much of a good thing can still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.880
become exhausting. The extended Berlin portion&nbsp;
of the plot of "Chapter 4" is where the length is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.880 --> 00:09:01.680
the most problematic. This segment of the film is&nbsp;
well-made but does not feel like an integral part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.680 --> 00:09:06.780
of the overall story. A new villain is introduced&nbsp;
and immediately killed off in the form of Killa,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:12.000
a fun character who unfortunately has no real&nbsp;
purpose in the story. The entire Berlin sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.480
could theoretically be left on the cutting&nbsp;
room floor without affecting the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:20.160
and the overall film would be much tighter and&nbsp;
better-paced as a result. It would also lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.044
a couple of new characters, which would&nbsp;
help "Chapter 4" feel less overstuffed.

00:09:24.044 --> 00:09:25.860
It should come as no surprise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:30.540
whatsoever that the best part of "John Wick:&nbsp;
Chapter 4" by far is the action. Every "John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:35.580
Wick" movie has had to raise the bar and find&nbsp;
new ways to showcase Mr. Wick's lethal skillset.&nbsp;

00:09:35.580 --> 00:09:40.260
"Chapter 4" continues to expand the styles of&nbsp;
action on offer by incorporating exciting new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:44.640
weapons like nunchucks and bows and arrows,&nbsp;
and by making one of its main players, Caine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:48.900
a blind assassin with a unique fighting&nbsp;
style. The introduction of Dragon's Breath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:53.820
shotgun shells for one spectacular sequence is&nbsp;
a fiery way to raise the shootout bar higher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:57.540
than it has ever been before. "Chapter 4" has&nbsp;
some shortcomings in the writing department,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.540 --> 00:10:01.267
but they are easy to overlook&nbsp;
when the action is so incredible.&nbsp;

